text.p:
I received your Letter, my dear Cousin, when he was in his worst state, for indeed he was very ill; much more so than I have ever seen him. It was a great Comfort to me to hear from yourself that you were so much better. I began to think this World would soon lose every charm for me. You cannot imagine anything more truly Sisterly than Mrs. Goodford's attention to us. She seemed to bring Comfort whenever I saw her and her very sensible feeling. Elizabeth was with us on Sunday Afternoon, though I much fear her Visit was shorter than she had kindly intended it should be, as she found Mr. Watts our Solicitor from Bath with us. He had just returned from London (where Mr. Hill had thought it necessary he should attend the Consultation of my Counsel) and brought the Brief with the Counsel's remarks for Parrot's perusal. This hour's attention to Business gave me a painful Proof of my dear Invalid's inability just now for things of this Nature;
he had no rest, although the Opiate which he had been obliged to take every Night never failed before to compose him. Thank God he was next day much better than I had imagined he would have been and has seemed gaining Strength every day since. From our very kind Friend Mr. Hill we understand Mr. Jekyll is very warm in the Business. So very black a Character as the Villain bears who is the Witness against me, one can only wonder at his Courage in exposing himself to the examination of a public Court and yet there seems to be so great a Combination of deep laid schemes, that I can only think with additional horror of what Art may do against Innocence . That the Set (for such I believe I may properly term them) have been thoroughly disappointed in not having a Sum of Money offered is most certain. Nay, I verily believe that even now they flatter themselves that my dear Husband's illness, and possible inability to attend me through such a scene may at the last oblige him to make proposals to them, but little do they know of either himself or me who could fancy such an Idea would ever arise in our breasts. I think I could die before I could do this. I confess I feel more shaken when I think how much this dear Invalid may suffer from the Trial, of fatigue incident to it, than I have ever done for myself, however that merciful God, who has supported us hitherto, will I trust not let us be overturned now. You were very good to say you wish to Attend me, had not your ill health prevented you. I can never doubt your Friendship for a Moment. Mr. Watts informed us that the Counsel wished to have Four of our Country Neighbours, and four of our Bath Acquaintances, to answer for me at Taunton. Relations would not be called upon for this Purpose. I should grieve to give any additional trouble to my kind Friends. One Lady, or at most two with me in Court would be all I ought to have. Your good Sister offered to come to me but it is a long Journey, and in her lame State, with the certainty of unavoidable inconveniences at Taunton, I cannot think of her coming. I have been disappointed in another quarter. My dear Affectionate Sister Austen, though in a state of health not equal to trials of any kind, has been with the greatest difficulty kept from me. In a letter from her a few days ago I had the pain to hear that her Valuable Son James having had his Horse fall with him by which his leg was broken. This is a loss indeed because he had been a perfect Son to me in Affection and his firm Friendship all through this trying Business had taught me to look to him and his Wife (a Relation of Lord Craven's well bred and sensible) to have come to us at the Assizes. Now I can neither ask Mother or Wife to leave him nor could I accept the Offer of my Nieces - to have two Young Creatures gazed at in a public Court would cut one to the very heart. Some of my kind Friends were early in their offers of attending us from Bath . It is a favour I never will first propose to anyone. If the kindness of nearer Friends than Berkshire ones is offered I shall with gratitude accept it. You see, my dear Cousin, this is the only Subject I can now write upon. Though I am highly pleased at our Friend Maria Goodford's account of the Connection
and Establishment of your handsome Mary , yet I have never said one word on the subject. I love you all to much not to be deeply interested in every Circumstance contributing to your Happiness, believe me. Pray remember us affectionately to Mrs. Cholmeley and those of my Young Cousins now at Easton. I had a letter from Carlton a little time ago with very kind offers from Mr. Welby of coming to Taunton if he could be of the least Service , but we declined the offer - indeed I know no use he could be of to answer so long a journey. If it had been within a very short distance the sight of Friends must have been Cheering at such a time - but Parrot thinks he shall have no time but for Lawyers. I am sure I shall have no thought but for him.
